# Describing the instrument: the Microscopes window and the Cameras window.
#
# Everything downstream keys on what is entered here, so most of these
# entries are tooltips rather than labels. They are long on purpose: this is
# a page you visit twice a year, and the questions it asks are ones you have
# to know some optics to answer.

#. ---- one turret position ------------------------------------------------
#. A row of the objective list. Empty is a legitimate state: real turrets
#. have gaps, and a five-position turret with three objectives must not
#. silently renumber itself.

#. Shown in the magnification box instead of "0.0×" when the position holds
#. no objective.
msgid "setup.objective.mag.state.empty"
msgstr "empty"

#. Shown in the aperture box instead of "NA 0.00" when the number is not
#. known. Two dashes rather than a word, because the box is 78 px wide and
#. the whole row of them has to stay readable as a column.
msgid "setup.objective.na.state.unknown"
msgstr "--"

#. An example of an objective's optical design, in the box that asks for
#. one. A real name rather than a description, because it is what is
#. engraved on the barrel and that is where a reader will look for it.
#. Achromat, Fluar or Neofluar would do as well.
msgid "setup.objective.kind.placeholder"
msgstr "Planapo"

#. ---- immersion ----------------------------------------------------------
#. What sits between the front element and the slide. These are the words
#. on the menu; the values they stand for are identifiers in the code and
#. do not change with the language.

msgid "setup.objective.immersion.dry.option"
msgstr "dry"

msgid "setup.objective.immersion.oil.option"
msgstr "oil"

msgid "setup.objective.immersion.water.option"
msgstr "water"

msgid "setup.objective.immersion.glycerol.option"
msgstr "glycerol"

#. ---- a capped position --------------------------------------------------

#. Offered only where it means something, which is a position with no
#. objective in it.
msgid "setup.objective.capped.label"
msgstr "capped"

msgid "setup.objective.capped.tooltip"
msgstr ""
"There is a dust cap in this position rather than an open hole.\n"
"\n"
"The two look like opposite ends of the scale: an open position passes\n"
"the whole condenser cone straight through and blows white, a capped\n"
"one is black. Both are simply 'no objective' unless you say which."

#. ---- the stand ----------------------------------------------------------

msgid "setup.scope.name.label"
msgstr "Name"

msgid "setup.scope.tube.label"
msgstr "Tube length"

#. The box carries a "mm" suffix the code supplies, so the tooltip does not
#. repeat the unit. The numbers are standards and manufacturers' own values.
msgid "setup.scope.tube.tooltip"
msgstr ""
"What turns an objective's magnification into a focal length:\n"
"f = tube / M, which is the focal length written into every capture.\n"
"\n"
"160 is the DIN finite standard and what most pre-1990 stands use. It is "
"engraved on the objective, under the\n"
"magnification. An infinity\n"
"stand has no tube length as such, so put its tube *lens* focal length\n"
"here instead: 165 Zeiss, 180 Olympus, 200 Nikon and Leica."

msgid "setup.scope.condenser.label"
msgstr "Condenser"

#. What the condenser is, in whatever words its owner would use. Examples
#. rather than a description, because the field is free text and there is no
#. right answer to describe.
msgid "setup.scope.condenser.placeholder"
msgstr "phase turret, darkfield stop"

msgid "setup.scope.condenser_na.label"
msgstr "Condenser NA"

#. Shown instead of "0.00" when the aperture has not been set. Not the same
#. as zero: it means assume the objective always wins.
msgid "setup.scope.condenser_na.state.unknown"
msgstr "unknown"

msgid "setup.scope.condenser_na.tooltip"
msgstr ""
"The condenser's *working* aperture, not the number engraved on it.\n"
"An NA 1.4 condenser only reaches 1.4 with oil between it and the slide;\n"
"dry, air caps it below 1.0, and the iris puts it anywhere below that.\n"
"\n"
"It sets how bright each objective is, because the smaller of the two\n"
"apertures gathers the light, and above about NA 0.5 that is the\n"
"condenser, not the objective. Used only as a first guess; a confirmed\n"
"rotation teaches the real value."

#. ---- the magnification changer ------------------------------------------
#. Rare glass, so it is off by default. Zeiss call theirs an Optovar; the
#. generic name is the label and the trade name is in the tooltip, where
#. somebody looking for it will find it. Both names are the manufacturers'
#. own and are worth keeping as they are.

msgid "setup.scope.changer.label"
msgstr "Magnification changer"

#. The tick box beside the factors, saying the stand has one at all.
msgid "setup.scope.changer.fitted.label"
msgstr "fitted"

#. On the tick box and on the factors beside it, which is why it describes
#. both. The factors box is read with a decimal point and spaces, so its own
#. example is not translated.
msgid "setup.scope.changer.tooltip"
msgstr ""
"An intermediate magnification changer, between the objective and\n"
"the tube lens. Zeiss call theirs an Optovar; Leitz, Olympus and\n"
"Nikon call it a magnification changer. Most stands have none.\n"
"\n"
"Its factors, space separated. It multiplies into total magnification\n"
"exactly as the relay does, and changes magnification without moving\n"
"the optical axis, which is what makes an overview frame cheap."

#. ---- which way round the image is ---------------------------------------

msgid "setup.scope.handedness.label"
msgstr "Image handedness"

msgid "setup.scope.handedness.normal.option"
msgstr "normal"

msgid "setup.scope.handedness.inverted.option"
msgstr "inverted"

msgid "setup.scope.handedness.tooltip"
msgstr ""
"How the image's handedness relates to the turret. Nothing to do with\n"
"whether your turret is conventional.\n"
"\n"
"Between the turret moving and a pixel darkening, the handedness\n"
"passes through the objective (which inverts the image), the head and any\n"
"photo tube, however the camera is screwed onto its C-mount, and the\n"
"raw stream arriving bottom-up. Four stages, each able to flip a sign.\n"
"\n"
"You should not need this: correcting a wrong proposal once teaches it."

#. ---- the turret ---------------------------------------------------------

#. The suffix on the count of turret positions, so the box reads "5
#. positions". The leading space separates it from the number and is part of
#. the string; a language that puts the noun first will want it at the other
#. end instead.
msgid "setup.turret.slots.detail"
msgstr " positions"

msgid "setup.turret.slots.tooltip"
msgstr ""
"How many positions the turret holds, including any that are empty.\n"
"\n"
"Four and five are usual; six and seven exist. It has to be the real\n"
"count, because stepping and turret detection both work in physical\n"
"order. A five-position turret described as four is one position out\n"
"for half the ring."

#. Beside the count, above the rows. No full stop: it is a caption rather
#. than a sentence.
msgid "setup.turret.order.detail"
msgstr "In turret order. The order matters for stepping and detection"

#. The section header above the rows. Upper case is how it is set; the code
#. does not raise it.
msgid "setup.turret.objectives.heading"
msgstr "OBJECTIVES"

#. ---- the camera and its relay -------------------------------------------
#. The relay travels with the camera rather than with the stand, because
#. that is what physically happens when a camera moves between scopes.

msgid "setup.camera.name.label"
msgstr "Name"

msgid "setup.camera.relay.label"
msgstr "Relay / adapter"

#. A real adapter, as an example of what to write. The make and the mount
#. are names; "1-2x" is its zoom range.
msgid "setup.camera.relay.placeholder"
msgstr "AmScope 1-2x C-mount"

msgid "setup.camera.relay_factor.label"
msgstr "Relay factor"

#. The box carries an "x" prefix the code supplies.
msgid "setup.camera.relay_factor.tooltip"
msgstr ""
"What the relay multiplies by, at the setting you use it.\n"
"\n"
"It sits between the objective and the sensor, so it multiplies into\n"
"total magnification exactly as a magnification changer does. A 1-2x\n"
"C-mount adapter left at 2x doubles the magnification at the sensor,\n"
"which changes the f-number and how much slide each pixel covers."

msgid "setup.camera.pixel.label"
msgstr "Pixel pitch"

#. Shown instead of "0.00 um" when the pitch has not been set, because zero
#. is what makes the program ask the camera for it rather than a pitch of
#. nothing.
msgid "setup.camera.pixel.state.ask"
msgstr "ask the camera"

#. IMX183 is a sensor part number.
msgid "setup.camera.pixel.tooltip"
msgstr ""
"Sensor pixel pitch, from the datasheet.\n"
"\n"
"Set it and every capture can record how much slide one pixel covers,\n"
"which is the number a scale bar is drawn from. The SDK is asked\n"
"first when this is left at zero, but it reports 0 on some models,\n"
"and a zero pitch means no scale bar anywhere, quietly. The IMX183\n"
"is 2.4 um."

msgid "setup.camera.serial.label"
msgstr "Serial"

msgid "setup.camera.serial.tooltip"
msgstr ""
"Read from the camera. This is how it recognises itself between sessions."

#. In place of the serial, for a camera that has never been plugged in.
msgid "setup.camera.serial.state.unseen"
msgstr "not seen yet"

#. ---- the Microscopes window ---------------------------------------------

msgid "setup.scopes.title"
msgstr "Microscopes"

msgid "setup.scopes.action.new"
msgstr "New…"

msgid "setup.scopes.action.remove"
msgstr "Remove"

#. The section header above the calibration key. Upper case is how it is
#. set; the code does not raise it.
msgid "setup.scopes.key.heading"
msgstr "CALIBRATION KEY"

msgid "setup.scopes.key.tooltip"
msgstr ""
"What a flat field is filed under. Change any of it and the existing flat "
"no longer applies."

#. The little window that asks for the name of a new stand.
msgid "setup.scopes.new.title"
msgstr "New microscope"

msgid "setup.scopes.new.label"
msgstr "Name"

#. Already filled in, so pressing return is enough. {number} is how many
#. stands the library will then hold.
msgid "setup.scopes.new.detail"
msgstr "Microscope {number}"

msgid "setup.scopes.remove.title"
msgstr "Remove microscope"

#. {name} is the stand being removed. The flats are not deleted, so the
#. sentence says so: it is what makes this safe to answer yes to.
msgid "setup.scopes.remove.detail"
msgstr ""
"Remove {name}? Flat fields filed under it stay on disk but will no longer "
"be found."

#. ---- the Cameras window -------------------------------------------------
#. There is no picker here on purpose. Which camera is in front of you is
#. whichever one is plugged in, matched by its serial, so this window is a
#. place to describe them rather than to choose between them.

msgid "setup.cameras.title"
msgstr "Cameras"

msgid "setup.cameras.action.remove"
msgstr "Remove"

msgid "setup.cameras.remove.tooltip"
msgstr "Forget this camera. It comes back if it is plugged in again."

msgid "setup.cameras.remove.title"
msgstr "Remove camera"

#. {name} is the camera being forgotten.
msgid "setup.cameras.remove.detail"
msgstr ""
"Forget {name}? It will be added again the next time it is plugged in."

#. The whole window, when the library is empty. Everything that appears gets
#. filed, so there is nothing to do here until something has appeared.
msgid "setup.cameras.empty.detail"
msgstr ""
"No camera has been seen yet.\n"
"\n"
"One is added the first time it is plugged in, recognised by its serial, "
"so there is nothing to describe until then."

#. {name} is the stand this camera was last used on.
msgid "setup.cameras.bound.detail"
msgstr "Last used on {name}."

msgid "setup.cameras.bound.detail.unused"
msgstr "Not yet used on a microscope."
